# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Docs — public by directory whitelist (policy changed 2026-08-18)
#
# WHY: `docs/` used to be ignored wholesale, which meant a task's documentation could never
# ride in the same MR as its code — the doc had to be written into a directory git refused to
# see. The rule now separates the two things that were conflated: WHAT a doc describes
# (features, bugs, decisions — publishable) from WHAT NUMBERS it carries (sales, analytics,
# account balances — never publishable).
#
# The list below is a WHITELIST on purpose. This repo is public: a push is an instant,
# irreversible publication that Google indexes and forks copy. A blacklist ("ignore the
# sensitive ones") fails open — a new doc with an ARPU table in it lands in git the moment
# somebody forgets. A whitelist fails closed: the worst case is a doc that did not make it
# into the MR, which is a nuisance, not an incident.
#
# The whitelist is only half the gate. `scripts/docs_leak_scan.py` reads every staged *.md
# and blocks the commit on business/analytics numbers, private dashboard links, third-party
# identities and credentials — see .github/workflows/docs-leak-guard.yml. Adding a directory
# here does NOT mean its contents were reviewed; it means the scanner now watches them.
#
# `docs/*` rather than `docs/`: git never descends into an excluded DIRECTORY, so a bare
# `docs/` would make every `!docs/...` line below unreachable.
docs/*

# Task-shaped documentation — the docs an MR is supposed to carry.
#
# This replaces the per-file allowlists added in 7aac7fe8 (`!docs/roadmap/ux-overhaul-roadmap.md`,
# `!docs/solutions/release-slice-…md`). Their reasoning was right — "~40 of ~190 solution docs
# quote revenue, so re-including the directory would publish all of it" — and it is exactly what
# the leak gate removes: the directory can be opened because every file in it is scanned, and a
# doc carrying revenue cannot be committed whether or not somebody remembered a line here. Both
# of those files stay tracked; both pass the scanner.
!docs/active/
!docs/archive/
!docs/backlog/
!docs/brainstorms/
!docs/completed/
!docs/decisions/
!docs/designs/
!docs/guidelines/
!docs/plans/
!docs/redesign/
!docs/reference/
!docs/roadmap/
!docs/solutions/
!docs/todos/
!docs/work/

# Technical runbooks referenced from CLAUDE.md — useless to a reader who cannot open them.
!docs/client-diagnostics.md
!docs/cloud-functions-diagnostics.md
!docs/navigation3-migration.md
!docs/adaptive-architecture.md
!docs/adaptive-decisions.md
!docs/README.md

# Markdown ONLY, and ONLY inside the directories named above — an allow-list of one extension
# rather than a deny-list of many. 311 MB of the old docs/ tree was video, screenshots and
# exported HTML: none of it is reviewable as text, and a binary committed once stays in history
# at full weight even after it is deleted. A deny-list also misses what has no extension at all —
# an early draft of this rule would have published `docs/solutions/*/.complete-stats`.
#
# ⚠️ The `docs/*/` prefix is load-bearing and must NOT be relaxed to `docs/**/`. The first
# version of this block read `docs/**/*` + `!docs/**/` + `!docs/**/*.md`, which re-includes any
# .md ANYWHERE under docs/ — making the 15 lines above decorative and turning the whole rule
# into the blacklist this policy exists to avoid. A brand-new `docs/analytics/revenue.md` and a
# top-level `docs/security-playbook.md` both landed as tracked-by-default under it.
# `docs/*/**` only ever reaches inside directories that `docs/*` did not exclude — i.e. exactly
# the whitelisted ones — so an unlisted directory stays invisible to git and the rule fails closed.
#
# Read as: re-exclude everything below a whitelisted directory (1), let git descend into its
# subdirectories so the un-ignore stays reachable (2), then re-include Markdown only (3).
docs/*/**
!docs/*/**/
!docs/*/**/*.md

# Never public regardless of where they are moved — these are the numbers themselves.
# (Belt-and-braces: none of them sit in a whitelisted directory today.)
docs/PRODUCT.md
docs/product-features.md
docs/unit-economics.md
docs/pricing-strategy.md
docs/marketing/
docs/reports/
docs/healthcheck/
docs/onboarding-screenshots/
# The store listing is the source of truth for Play Console copy and has been tracked since
# before this policy; the directory around it is screenshots. Re-open the directory so git can
# descend into it, then close everything in it except that one file.
!docs/store-screenshots/
docs/store-screenshots/*
!docs/store-screenshots/store-listing-en.md
SECURITY.md
SECURITY_DELIVERY_SUMMARY.txt

